  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slow-moving front passes through today bringing \nstorm chances to portions of the area. \n\nEarly morning radar shows a few light showers persisting across the \nNorthern Neck, Middle Peninsula, and Eastern Shore. They should \ndissipate and move offshore in the next hour or two, but don't \nbe surprised if you get a few spits of rain if you're in these \nlocations this morning. Surface analysis currently has a cold \nfront draped across the Northeast down across the Ohio River \nValley and into the eastern Plains. This front is expected to \ncontinue slowly dropping south across our local area through the\nday today. Since this front will take most, if not the whole \nday to pass through, raised high temperatures a couple of \ndegrees for most everyone. The forecast now has highs topping \nout in the mid 80s across the far north with upper 80s to 90 \ndegrees for the I-64 corridor and south into NE NC. Dewpoints \nwill be in the lower 70s, so it will feel a bit sticky outside \nwith heat indices in the lower to mid 90s across the south. \nDewpoints will try to drop into the 60s for northern locations \novernight as high pressure briefly extends down from \nsoutheastern Canada, allowing for low temperatures to bottom \nout in the mid 60s. Upper 60s are expected across central and \nsouthern localities, with 70s along the coast. \n\nWith the front over VA today, it will be a moister airmass in \nplace. PWATS are currently rising to over 2\" as of this writing \nand could reach 2.25\" across the S/SE this afternoon. Scattered \nshowers and storms are forecast to develop this afternoon into \nthe evening hours. Hi-res guidance continues to focus on our \nsouthwestern counties (Prince Edward, Lunenburg, Mecklenburg, \netc) having the better chance at storms. SPC has nudged a \nMarginal Risk for severe weather up against southwestern \nMecklenburg County. While the threat looks fairly low due to \nshear and CAPE values being unimpressive, can't totally rule out\nan isolated strong wind gust if storms are able to become \norganized. With the front not expected to totally clear our \nforecast area, there could be some lingering showers and storms \ninto the late evening and early overnight hours.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Looking more unsettled and potentially cooler next \nweek as low pressure develops. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front will drop south of our area into NC/SC \non Saturday. Broad surface high pressure will move east across \nsoutheastern Canada and New England before sliding offshore Sunday \nmorning. This will allow for a mostly benign mid-September\nSaturday with high temperatures in the upper 70s to around 80 \ndegrees for western locations and the Eastern Shore, with lower \n80s for eastern VA and northeast NC. Northeasterly winds could \nbecome a bit breezy for eastern and coastal locations. This \neast/northeasterly surface flow will also keep cloud cover \noverhead through the day. Can't totally rule out an isolated \nshower, but chances are on the low end. Winds will turn more \nsoutherly on Wednesday allowing for warmer air to return. Highs \nwill be in the mid to upper 80s. Could start to see some rain \nchances push back into northern counties late in the day as\nshortwaves ride along the flow.\n\nA more unsettled pattern sets its eyes on the eastern US for the new \nwork week. At the surface, a large ridge of high pressure will set \nup over eastern Canada and into New England. This will keep \npersistent onshore flow going, resulting in continuing rain chances \neach day. If this comes to fruition, Monday may be our last day with \nwidespread temperatures in the 80s for the forecast period. By late \nTuesday into Wednesday, models agree that low pressure will develop \naloft in the Great Lakes Region before potentially churning down \ninto the northern Mid-Atlantic. Meanwhile at the surface, we \ncould be dealing with a coastal low pressure developing offshore\nas well. This would create windy conditions across our marine \nand coastal locations, possibly for multiple days. Coastal \nflooding will also be something to watch by mid-week thanks to \nthe elevated consistent onshore flow. WPC has added a Slight \nRisk of Excessive Rainfall for southern/southeastern portions of\nthe area both Monday and Tuesday as moisture will be in \nabundance and there won't be much flow to make things move much.\nWPC is also leaning hard on a cold air damming situation \nkeeping high temperatures in the 60s Wednesday (70s near the \ncoast), but think that may be a bit too low for the current time\nof year. Regardless, there's agreement that temperatures will \nlikely drop below normal for mid to late week with rain chances \nsticking around.",
